WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.140
- Hi there.
I'm Seth Juarez, and I'm chatting

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:12.020
with Microsoft
Research's Katja Hofmann

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.510
and Evelyne Viegas about Project Malmo,

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.260
an AI testing environment

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.530
based upon Minecraft.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:19.870
Welcome!
- Hi, Seth.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:21.070
- How's everybody doing today?

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.630
- Fantastic.
Great day.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:26.230
- So, I heard about this topic recently,

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.930
and it absolutely was
really impressive, and here's why.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:33.500
Like I was talking before,
I talked to Yoshua Bengio about deep learning,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.360
and one of the things he said is that
one of the state-of-the-art things

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.470
that he wants to do
is he wants to use

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.940
deep learning for exploratory AI.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:46.040
So, why don't you talk about Project Malmo
and why this is a perfect piece of software for that.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.220
Why don't we start with you?

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.170
- Absolutely.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.680
We really see Minecraft as one of those stepping stones

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.760
that are going to allow us
to push the state of the art in AI

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.490
and eventually work towards more
complex real-world applications.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:03.350
Two of the things that I'm really excited
about that we can do in Malmo,

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.020
which is based on Minecraft,

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:08.990
is that first we can invest
in this kind of life-long learnings.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:13.860
So, coming up with approaches
that bootstrap from smaller,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.850
kind of simpler tasks
that learn common-sense knowledge

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:20.630
and then build that up to eventually
solve harder and harder tasks.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:25.100
The other part that I'm really excited
about is this concept of collaborative AI,

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.210
so what happens when you put more
than one agent into an environment.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:34.680
For example, how can AI agents learn to collaborate,
and what happens when you put a human in there?

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.600
Can they learn to figure out
what the human is trying to achieve

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.010
and how they can help them
achieve their goal?

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:40.940
- Well, Project Malmo.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.400
Tell us about it from your perspective.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.970
- Sure, so this is a very exciting project,

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:49.520
and I actually want to bring right now

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:50.970
the collaborative aspect

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.790
that Katja was talking about.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.770
In fact, when we started the project,

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:58.870
when Katja started with the researchers
working on this project,

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:02.880
we started interacting with
the external community upfront,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.000
just to understand first what was the need,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.480
what were the requirements.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:14.470
And we did a few what we call some technology days,
just to figure out, to listen to the community.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:18.220
And pretty soon, we figured out
that actually it would be fantastic

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.870
if we could work more closely
with the community,

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.560
which ended up bringing Malmo, this platform,

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.020
to the open source.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:33.790
And since then, actually, we've had
thousands of people who've been using it.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:37.130
- That's amazing, so you started upfront by thinking,

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:40.080
"We're just going to put this out."
What have people been doing?

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.080
Because you mentioned thousands.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:43.540
What have people been doing with this technology?

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:44.010
- Absolutely.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:49.600
So, we were planning this as a tool
for the research community from the start

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.780
and really thinking about how such a platform

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.780
could advance the state of the art in AI.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:57.550
One thing we hadn't really anticipated
was the level of enthusiasm

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:01.170
we got from outside
the research community.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:06.230
We get high school students
who learn to program on the Malmo platform,

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.560
and that's really exciting for them,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.210
because you can actually see what an algorithm does

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:12.440
when you use it to steer an agent.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:19.530
We get a lot of interest
from university professors, who use it in their courses

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.030
to teach students
about concepts in AI

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:23.890
and programming and reinforcement learning.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:26.340
So, there's a huge diversity of users.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.170
A lot of times, we only see
the visitor numbers on GitHub.

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:33.200
We see that there's a lot of people,
but there's a lot bubbling under the surface

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.690
that we're not even aware of.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:39.440
So, if you have some cool users for Malmo
that you would like to tell us about,

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:40.510
definitely get in touch.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:42.430
We're really eager to hear about that.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.760
- What's the most surprising thing
you've seen people use Project Malmo for?

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.830
- Gosh, I'll tell you first who is
the most surprising person I've seen.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:49.940
- Let's do that, yeah.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:58.020
- There was a sixth grader once who actually
came to my office and was completely inspired.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:02.500
He was doing a visit as part of some
students' gathering at Microsoft,

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:06.110
and he had heard about Malmo.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:08.420
Actually, he had really heard about Minecraft, right?

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:09.790
[ Laughter ] - Of course.
Who hasn't?

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:11.080
- He was really into Minecraft.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:14.960
But then, he heard about Malmo and was like, wow.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.920
So, he started to interact actually
with the researchers and say, you know,

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:22.220
"Wow, now I can actually
have some AI agents.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.190
I can play with it, I can learn about it."

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:27.890
And so that was to me probably
the most surprising person

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:30.140
trying to use Malmo,

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:34.290
also showing that the bar
of entry can be as low

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.800
or as high as we want it to be.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:37.450
- And that's amazing.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:42.060
Were they actually coding these things
to go around and mine stuff and build stuff,

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.460
or what was this
sixth grader actually doing?

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.130
Just interest, or what were they doing?

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.250
- I think they were trying to do
a little bit of both, right?

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:52.590
The first thing, they were really trying to learn
about like, what's this whole agents?

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:54.390
How can I start coding?

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:57.570
But actually, the person started coding, actually.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:02.010
So, yes, it was really
an environment to experiment,

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:06.060
to have fun with the characters
he was very familiar with.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.170
- Let's talk about a test bed like Project Malmo.

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:10.800
Why is it an important thing?

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.670
- Well, in real-world applications --
which, in the long run,

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:18.020
we really want to enable
real-world applications.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.370
We're not doing this for the sake
of learning to play Minecraft.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.980
I mean, if that's what we need, we have
people who are pretty good at this.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:31.950
But what we really want is to isolate certain aspects
of this whole huge problem of AI

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:34.620
and start to make progress
in those specific dimensions.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:40.620
And in a simulated environment like this,
we can specifically construct the tasks

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.400
that allow us to really push on
those dimensions.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:48.970
For example, if someone wants to do
research on multimodal approaches

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:50.690
that integrate language

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:52.850
and visual aspects
of an environment,

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:55.840
this is something you can
set up very easily in Malmo.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:59.770
And it allows you to really focus
on the learning approach itself

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:03.160
instead of having to do all this data collection

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:05.400
setting up a task in the real world,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:06.720
which can be much slower.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:12.020
Eventually, we see this research
to feed into the real-world applications,

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:15.170
but first we can push,
really understand what's going on.

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:17.720
- Why do you think it's such an important test bed?

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:23.010
- Well, I think it's really important
because I'm going to go back to the community.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:27.400
Actually, I want to talk about collaborative
AI challenge that we've done recently.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.360
- Oh, interesting.
- This is one way,

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:30.800
as Katja was mentioning earlier,

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.320
really the goal of the platform --

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.610
not just doing the platform
for the sake of doing it --

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:36.910
is really to help advance
the state of the art,

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:38.330
to get people engaged.

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:43.030
Also, to use it for people maybe
to just learn, like PhD students,

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.740
but also some enthusiasts
actually are interested in learning

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:47.740
a bit about all these approaches.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:51.510
So, the team put together an AI challenge.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:58.100
I think Katja will be describing it
a bit later on in more detail, but I want to emphasize

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:01.430
that the interesting part
about that challenge

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:03.480
is that it's really collaborative.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:08.190
This is all really about collaborative AI,
and I think that's something we're trying to do

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:10.750
which is different
from what is out there.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:15.930
And Project Malmo actually is ideal for that,
which is bringing people together

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.420
to try to do some specific task,

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:19.950
like about creating something.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:24.750
Or in the context of the challenge
that we had, this was actually a task

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:27.490
about trying to catch a pig together.

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:30.220
[ Laughter ]
- I feel like we've talked about this a lot.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:32.640
Can you show us a little demo on how this works?

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:34.080
What do you got?
- Absolutely.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:36.390
I think you can actually see this on the screen there.

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:39.680
- Totally can.
- What I have here is the task

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.020
that we set out
as the collaborative AI challenge.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:46.460
We are going to be a human agent
interacting with another agent,

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:49.980
and the task of the students
was to actually create an agent

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:53.210
that would take the role
that we're playing here.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:55.200
- OK.
- So, you can see here,

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:56.980
we're getting ready to catch the pig.

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:01.790
There are two agents in this small enclosure here,
and we are controlling the red agent here,

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:03.820
and there's another one here
in the corner.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:07.040
To catch the pig, we actually have to corner it,
so we have to collaborate.

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:12.160
But that will only work if the other agent
is actually able to collaborate with us.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.900
- Sure.
- So, let's see if they're a good collaborator.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:18.010
- So, you're the red one,
and the blue one is the actual AI one.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:18.790
- Exactly.

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:23.940
You can see how I'm controlling this one over here,
and you can already see that the other agent

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:25.150
is moving towards the pig,

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:26.590
so that's a pretty good indication

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:29.240
that they're actually trying
to collaborate with us.

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:34.960
And this inference, this kind of understanding
what the other agent's intentions are

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:36.830
and how our plan,

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:40.170
our behavior, can fit in to coordinate with them,

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:42.220
that is really the core
of this challenge.

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:45.940
If the other agent were to move around randomly
or were trying to trick us,

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:50.130
then we should draw
a different conclusion.

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:51.840
We shouldn't try to collaborate with them.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.690
- So, this is interesting.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:57.470
Because I'm looking at this, and I can think --
because I'm kind of a programmer, too.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:00.440
Is this- like, are people writing
deterministic algorithms for them?

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:01.370
Are they probabilistic?

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:03.430
Are they going full AI machine learning?

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:05.710
What are they doing for this, Katja?

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:06.680
- Fantastic question.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:09.500
So, we actually had 83 teams

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.210
from around the world participating

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:13.180
in this first version
of the challenge,

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.000
and what we saw in the winning teams

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.670
was that there was a huge variety

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:19.200
in terms of the approaches
that people tried.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.010
Some people really went down
the deep reinforcement learning route

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:24.470
and had some great insights

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:26.800
into how to learn efficiently

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:30.470
in this kind of more complex
modeling environment.

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:32.630
Others went the classic AI route.

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:36.250
Some spent a lot of time
really manually modeling this environment.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:40.470
Some went via belief modeling
and probabilistic inference.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:43.840
And at the moment, what we saw was that
a lot of those approaches

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:45.620
were kind of on par
with each other.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:49.680
So, what I'm looking forward
to is seeing how those different communities,

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:51.430
people with those
different backgrounds,

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:54.490
can learn from each other,
maybe integrate different ideas,

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:57.590
and use that to come up
with more new,

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:01.120
innovative approaches to solving
those kinds of collaborative tasks.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:03.070
- What surprised you
about this challenge?

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:06.470
Because I'm sure you put this out there,
and you're like, "I wonder what's going to happen,"

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:07.960
and then you got
a lot of a response.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:09.510
What was your reaction to this?

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:12.970
- Well, it was fantastic to see
so many people collaborate.

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:17.450
As Katja was mentioning, so the task itself
seemed kind of simple,

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:19.750
but actually what was
really interesting

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.120
when we had the winners

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.360
come and talk about
their different approaches...

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:28.710
First of all, actually, some of them
just collaborated just to present their approach,

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:31.650
so that speaks to the point
of their approaches;

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:33.650
some of them were
not that different.

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:39.940
And the other aspect also very interesting,
I thought, was- Actually, even for us,

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:43.110
trying to learn how people
are collaborating,

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:45.260
and some of them had some approach

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:49.130
they were trying to kind of trick
the pig, so to speak.

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:50.680
[ Laughter ]
- That's funny.

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:56.490
- It's also learning a lot
about how agents, whether humans or AI agents,

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:58.310
are also trying to collaborate

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:02.470
to achieve a specific task

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:04.800
and solve a problem, so that was really interesting.

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:10.240
Beyond the challenge, I think it's also
seeing what motivates people,

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:11.550
what are the incentives,

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:15.600
so it's learning a lot about that too
as we design our challenges.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:19.200
- As I'm looking at this, if I was going
to perform part of this challenge,

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:23.350
I would have to program the agent
to help me corner the pig, effectively.

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:27.000
- Exactly.
- So, I'm a programmer.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.110
How do I write this?

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:33.390
Because if I remember right,
it's written in Java, or at least Minecraft is.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.530
But can you use any kind of language?

00:11:36.530 --> 00:11:37.600
How do I get started?

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:38.970
What do I do to make this work?

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:40.590
- Fantastic question.

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:46.930
We from the start defined Project
Malmo to be as open as possible,

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:49.230
so we didn't want
to exclude any audiences.

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:53.310
We put a lot of effort into making
this platform actually cross-platform,

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:56.540
so it runs on Linux, Windows, Mac OS,

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:59.590
and people can use their
favorite programming language

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:01.290
to start and code agents.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:08.350
Project Malmo essentially
provides an API that hooks into the Minecraft game,

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:10.050
so it uses the modeling framework

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:12.100
to get information

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:13.680
about the state of the world

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:16.610
and present commands to Minecraft.

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:21.320
But our platform takes away all the complexity
of actually interacting with the Minecraft game,

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:25.430
so that researchers can just focus
on starting to write code

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:27.740
of what the agent
should observe,

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:30.590
how it should interpret
those observations,

00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:34.440
and how it should consequently
try to act in the world.

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:40.450
The languages we support include C,
C++, Java, Python, Lua, so there's a lot for everyone.

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:41.710
- I also saw there's a C#
one.

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:42.890
That's my favorite one.

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:44.890
- [ Laughing ] Absolutely.

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:47.200
- When I'm writing this stuff, am I...

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:50.740
I'm trying to understand what this looks like,
because it feels like there's some kind of wrapper

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:52.390
that I'm sending information to,

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:54.170
and then I'm querying
for information.

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:56.570
Is that the whole of it?

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:57.780
What do you say?

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.620
- Yes, absolutely.
- Yeah, so you really can think

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:02.580
about this as this interactive loop

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.610
between the agent on one side
and the world,

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:07.560
the Minecraft environment, on the other.

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:11.620
For the person writing the agent,
that looks quite simply

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:14.250
as you're pulling a state
from the environment,

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:15.700
and then you send an action back.

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:20.290
So, you can really start to think
about this kind of interactive loop

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:21.630
that is often used,
for example,

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:22.900
in reinforcement learning

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:26.300
but that also matches a lot
of different interactive scenarios

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:29.500
where agents have to generally learn
to interact with the world.

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:33.370
